"John H." <Joh...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Derek Ray <de...@moot.its.only.a.spamtrap.org> wrote:
>> John H. <Joh...@gmail.com> wrote:

>>> Boots of Stepguarding

>> They need to display a message when they do this
>> and force a --More--; otherwise people will bang
>> on the keys and think they're lagged when
>> actually the boots are stopping them.

> Interesting, but potentially annoying if there are
> a lot of traps around.  I'd think a message but no
> --more-- forcing.

Well, the intent of Derek's "--More--" was to make
the other (travel) keystrokes be _ignored_ rather
than counting against the PC, until the player stops
being braindead and presses the space bar. It's an
intercept, as much as a prompt. Except in the couple
of trap-rife places in the game, the "--More--"
would be a plenty cheap price for the boots'
behavior, and even there, hey, it's still not as bad
as the #untrap interface keystroke count.

>>> These boots do not take the player's state into
>>> account,

>> This will piss me off in combination with my ring
>> of levitation.

> I'm thinking that levitation should be taken into
> account, since it's a physical state.  The idea is
> that the boots consider the ground, so if the
> player is floating they do not operate.

Works for me.

>>> Boots of Wandering

>> Has no effect; skilled players currently BUC-ID all boots before putting
>> them on anyway, to avoid the boots of fumbling.

So? Make them autocurse, like several other items
do. Playing foolishly should _sting_, and this would
get rid of a lot of cheap-ID "try on the boots and
see if you can kick a rock farther", "try on the
boots and see if you can now jump", stuff by making
trying on uncursed unIDed boots screamingly
dangerous.

>> (Also, one in ten moves isn't really that much.)

And every once in a while, even though he's
ascended ever class, Derek says something that makes
one wonder if he's ever played the game _at all_.

One step in ten that you step into the lava you're
walking alongside? One step in ten that you step
into the swamp through which you're threading on the
dry land? One time in ten that you step off the
drawbridge and into the moat? One in ten is _huge_.

